\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Example}
\title{Vanishing off the stabilizer}
\date{2026-11-21}

\p{Let #{B} be the indicator of #{G^\circ}: the value #{1} on the
stabilizer and the value #{0} off it. It is a double-coset
kernel, because the identity double coset is #{G^\circ}. On the
space of functions constant on double cosets, multiplication by
#{B} fixes the identity indicator and sends every other indicator
to #{0}. For any class-function kernel, #{M_B} therefore retains
only that coordinate, and the image of
#{\transferop=M_B\,C_{\mathcal K}\,M_B} on this space lies in the
span of the identity indicator.}
